# Default handlers, applied by .chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_darwin-duti.sh.
# Measured on macOS 26.6: 41 of the 51 extensions below actually take effect.
#
# Only extensions that resolve to a real UTI can be bound at all. An extension
# no app declares gets a synthesized dyn.* identifier, and LaunchServices
# refuses handlers for those with error -50. VS Code's Info.plist declares 153
# extensions; anything outside that set, and outside the system UTIs, has
# nothing to bind.
#
# 28 such extensions were removed from this file (bom cfm cfml cnf conf csr
# data dist fields haml hotkey htaccess inf keylayout kmlibrary manifest msass
# opml orig pg rd rdoc readme rhtml sb sts textmate tmp). A sample file for
# each resolves to no opening application whatsoever, so double-clicking one
# gets the "choose an application" dialog. The public.plain-text line does NOT
# rescue them: an unknown extension is typed as dyn.* conforming to
# public.data, not to plain text. To bind one, do it by hand in Finder via
# Open With > Change All — it will not be reproducible from this file.
#
# A run still prints ~18 "error -50" lines, and most are cosmetic. Nine of
# them (.coffee .ctp .erb .gemspec .jsx .less .rst .scss .tex) bind correctly
# via their real UTI while duti additionally tries a dyn variant that is
# refused — those extensions do work despite the message. The other nine come
# from the contested block at the bottom.

# plain text files without an extension
com.microsoft.VSCode public.plain-text all

# executable scripts
com.microsoft.VSCode public.unix-executable all

